1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,957
You ask a coding agent for one thing, and
then you wait.

2
00:00:04,307 --> 00:00:08,446
New ideas come up, but the agent can only
do one thing at a time.

3
00:00:08,796 --> 00:00:12,572
So you interrupt it, or you try to
remember everything yourself.

4
00:00:14,072 --> 00:00:15,779
symcc fixes that.

5
00:00:16,129 --> 00:00:17,985
It is a plugin for Claude Code.

6
00:00:18,335 --> 00:00:23,220
You talk to one lead, in your normal chat,
and you can also act on the board panel.

7
00:00:23,570 --> 00:00:27,801
The lead listens, writes your ideas down
on a shared task board,

8
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:30,247
and splits big jobs into small tasks.

9
00:00:30,597 --> 00:00:34,011
Then workers pick up the tasks and do them
side by side.

10
00:00:34,361 --> 00:00:36,996
Each worker has its own copy of your code,

11
00:00:36,996 --> 00:00:40,825
so your own branch stays untouched until
something is merged.

12
00:00:41,175 --> 00:00:44,631
The lead knows the whole picture because
you keep talking with it.

13
00:00:44,981 --> 00:00:49,350
For each task, it prepares just what that
worker needs: what to build,

14
00:00:49,350 --> 00:00:51,722
why, which files, and how to check it.

15
00:00:52,072 --> 00:00:54,035
So every worker starts focused.

16
00:00:54,385 --> 00:00:57,201
The lead also works like a project
manager.

17
00:00:57,551 --> 00:00:59,505
It sets priorities and order,

18
00:00:59,505 --> 00:01:03,481
updates tasks when plans change, and
retries or drops them.

19
00:01:03,831 --> 00:01:06,135
You talk, and it keeps the plan in order.

20
00:01:06,485 --> 00:01:09,237
It also acts like a responsible team lead.

21
00:01:09,587 --> 00:01:14,323
It understands what matters, and when
something is unclear, it asks you a
question.

22
00:01:14,673 --> 00:01:18,287
Because the workers do the hands-on work
in their own copies,

23
00:01:18,287 --> 00:01:22,908
the lead's own conversation stays clean,
and it is always free to talk to you.

24
00:01:24,408 --> 00:01:25,752
The board is simple.

25
00:01:26,102 --> 00:01:28,363
A task is in one of four places.

26
00:01:28,713 --> 00:01:30,697
Todo, doing, review, or done.

27
00:01:31,047 --> 00:01:35,186
You can see at a glance what is running,
what is waiting, and what needs you.

28
00:01:35,536 --> 00:01:37,499
And the lead is smart about order.

29
00:01:37,849 --> 00:01:40,878
If one task needs the result of another,
it waits.

30
00:01:41,228 --> 00:01:45,111
If two tasks change the same files, they
go one after the other.

31
00:01:45,461 --> 00:01:47,637
Everything else runs at the same time.

32
00:01:47,987 --> 00:01:49,928
You can change your mind any time.

33
00:01:50,278 --> 00:01:52,817
Just tell the lead, do task six first.

34
00:01:53,167 --> 00:01:55,577
And you can always see what was done, and
why.

35
00:01:55,927 --> 00:02:00,330
Every task shows where it came from, with
a small symbol: your words,

36
00:02:00,330 --> 00:02:02,882
a scout, a worker's report, or the lead.

37
00:02:03,232 --> 00:02:05,701
It also keeps the history of its tries,

38
00:02:05,701 --> 00:02:09,056
the worker's report, and the time and
tokens it cost.

39
00:02:10,556 --> 00:02:12,839
Each worker gets its own git worktree.

40
00:02:13,189 --> 00:02:16,290
That is just a separate folder with its
own branch,

41
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:18,479
so workers never step on each other.

42
00:02:18,829 --> 00:02:21,240
By default, three workers run at once.

43
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:25,184
Each task gets a worker type that fits it:
general,

44
00:02:25,184 --> 00:02:28,566
docs, refactor, researcher, security, or
tester.

45
00:02:28,916 --> 00:02:31,662
You can build the team your project needs
by

46
00:02:31,662 --> 00:02:34,783
adding or changing role files in the roles
folder.

47
00:02:35,133 --> 00:02:37,394
A task lists the files it may change.

48
00:02:37,744 --> 00:02:40,965
If a worker changes anything else, it is
not merged.

49
00:02:41,315 --> 00:02:43,769
Before any merge, your tests must pass.

50
00:02:44,119 --> 00:02:47,489
If they fail, a new worker sees the error
and tries again.

51
00:02:47,839 --> 00:02:49,674
You choose how much to trust it.

52
00:02:50,024 --> 00:02:54,092
In auto mode, a finished task that passes
is merged right away,

53
00:02:54,092 --> 00:02:56,934
except tasks the lead marks as needs
review,

54
00:02:56,934 --> 00:02:59,581
which it picks for you to check yourself.

55
00:02:59,931 --> 00:03:03,973
In review mode, you open the task on the
board, look at the changes,

56
00:03:03,973 --> 00:03:06,886
and approve it there, or reject it with a
reason.

57
00:03:07,236 --> 00:03:11,695
And if a worker has a question, the task
waits under a heading called needs you.

58
00:03:12,045 --> 00:03:13,879
You answer, and it goes on.

59
00:03:15,379 --> 00:03:16,659
Then there are scouts.

60
00:03:17,009 --> 00:03:19,676
A scout is a helper that wakes up on a
timer.

61
00:03:20,026 --> 00:03:23,525
It looks for bugs, missing tests, or old
dependencies.

62
00:03:23,875 --> 00:03:26,541
It can only read, never change your files.

63
00:03:26,891 --> 00:03:29,558
When it finds something, it reports to the
lead.

64
00:03:29,908 --> 00:03:33,748
The lead turns the finding into a task,
and a worker picks it up.

65
00:03:34,098 --> 00:03:35,805
This is autonomous coding.

66
00:03:36,155 --> 00:03:38,435
It keeps working while you are not there,

67
00:03:38,435 --> 00:03:40,827
and you come back to finished, tested
work.

68
00:03:42,327 --> 00:03:44,119
Let us follow one request.

69
00:03:44,469 --> 00:03:48,010
You type, add a search command, and make
the errors friendlier.

70
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:50,024
The lead makes three tasks.

71
00:03:50,374 --> 00:03:51,611
The search index.

72
00:03:51,961 --> 00:03:54,713
The search command, which waits for the
index.

73
00:03:55,063 --> 00:03:56,514
And the friendlier errors.

74
00:03:56,864 --> 00:03:59,616
The index and the errors start now, in
parallel.

75
00:03:59,966 --> 00:04:03,209
Each worker builds in its own copy, and
the tests run.

76
00:04:03,559 --> 00:04:05,543
The first task lands in review.

77
00:04:05,893 --> 00:04:09,519
You open it on the board, see the changes,
and press approve.

78
00:04:09,869 --> 00:04:12,344
It merges, and the search command starts.

79
00:04:12,694 --> 00:04:14,571
You only talked to the lead once.

80
00:04:14,921 --> 00:04:16,756
The rest was a click on the board.

81
00:04:18,256 --> 00:04:22,864
symcc is free and open source, under the
MIT license.

82
00:04:23,214 --> 00:04:24,878
You need Claude Code and git.

83
00:04:25,228 --> 00:04:30,135
Install it as a plugin with one command,
and the steps are in the project's page.

84
00:04:30,485 --> 00:04:33,983
Set your test command, ask for something,
and watch the board.

85
00:04:34,333 --> 00:04:35,443
symcc.

86
00:04:35,793 --> 00:04:37,499
Many agents, one board.

87
00:04:37,849 --> 00:04:40,964
Autonomous coding that keeps working while
you are away.
